From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Campos Ramos <Capitain_Jack@yahoo.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 05:22:13 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 5/5] 3D viewer: the stereo separation is the person's depth
 limits

The two eyes are as far apart as the person's depth limits allow, and no
farther: the desktop gives the 3D view's window the limits set once for the
screen (Stereo KWin's 3D Depth settings, read through libstereo-declare in the
pixels of the view), a first pass from the camera between the eyes reads how
near and how far the pixels of the scene are, and the separation is the
largest under which every one of them stays inside the limits.  The limits
are read again four times a second while the view shows 3D.  Without limits
the eyes are the same picture and the info bar says why, once.

The eye separation setting is gone.  The view's 3D options are "Enable 3D",
on unless the person turns it off, and a button to Display Settings, where
each screen's 3D mode, output and eye order are chosen.  On a screen with no
stereo visual the view stays one picture without a word; it is said only
when the person ticks Enable 3D there.

With WXTRACE=KI_TRACE_EDA_3D_CANVAS each frame's limits, depth range,
look-at distance, separation and the disparity of its nearest and farthest
pixel are traced.

Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5 via Claude Code
